+builtOn:
+  sequenceIds: [swh-seq-s5-duckweed]
+  lookForIds: [swh-begin-own-question, swh-begin-kept, swh-tests-follows-question, swh-obs-separate, swh-claim-arguable, swh-claim-individual, swh-evidence-justifies, swh-evidence-selected, swh-evidence-anomalies, swh-reading-peers-first, swh-reading-not-answer-key, swh-reading-something-changes, swh-reflect-has-original, swh-reflect-about-thinking]
+  misapplicationIds: [mis-swh-supplied-question, mis-swh-evidence-recopied, mis-swh-answer-key, mis-swh-no-negotiation, mis-swh-lost-beginnings, mis-swh-closed-practical]
+guideSection: checklist
+items:
+  - id: practical
+    type: choice
+    at: beginning-ideas
+    processing: Analysis
+    kind: Choose the practical
+    tests: design
+    title: "Your routine: Year 9, six lessons inside a sustainability unit. Many students think pollution always makes living things grow less."
+    q: Which practical gives SWH the most to work with?
+    options:
+      - text: Counting the fronds on one duckweed plant under a hand lens
+        fb: One right answer, so claims can't differ and the reading phase has nothing to do. The guide calls this using SWH on a closed practical.
+      - text: Following a printed method to grow duckweed in fertiliser and in plain water
+        fb: The method and the comparison are set, so students' own questions have nowhere to go.
+      - text: Duckweed in cups with run-off substances students choose from an approved list, and the question of what run-off does to a creek
+        fb: Yes. Students' questions can differ, fertiliser's result surprises them, and “impact” has to be argued about the whole waterway. The reading phase has a real disagreement to work on.
+      - text: Researching water pollution online and writing a summary
+        fb: There's no practical, so there are no observations of their own to make a claim from.
+    answer: 2
+    refs:
+      misapplicationIds: [mis-swh-closed-practical]
+
+  - id: rubric
+    type: concept
+    at: beginning-ideas
+    processing: Retrieval
+    kind: Before you build
+    title: What the report will look for
+    lead: >-
+      The board checks your plan against the SWH guide's look-fors and misapplications. A card counts only in the
+      phase whose job it does, and some cards look efficient but bring a known problem with them. Leaving out the
+      reading phase, where claims meet other students, is the cut the guide warns about most.
+    rows:
+      - phaseId: beginning-ideas
+        does: Students' own questions, written down and kept.
+        move: "Watch for: a question on the board."
+      - phaseId: tests
+        does: A method that answers their question.
+        move: "Watch for: one method for everyone."
+      - phaseId: observations
+        does: What happened, apart from what it means.
+        move: "Watch for: explaining while recording."
+      - phaseId: claims
+        does: An individual claim someone could disagree with.
+        move: "Watch for: one claim per group."
+      - phaseId: evidence
+        does: Chosen data, and why it supports the claim.
+        move: "Watch for: the counting table again."
+      - phaseId: reading
+        does: Peers first, then the text as another voice.
+        move: "Watch for: the text as the answer key."
+      - phaseId: reflection
+        does: Beginning ideas handed back; how thinking changed.
+        move: "Watch for: a summary of the topic."
+
+  - id: board
+    type: build
+    at: tests
+    processing: Knowledge utilisation
+    kind: Build the plan
+    tests: design
+    title: Build your SWH routine.
+    brief:
+      who: Year 9 · Environmental sustainability · six lessons inside the unit
+      text: "The practical: duckweed in cups, run-off substances from an approved list, and the question of what run-off does to a creek."
+    q: Place one activity in each phase. Not every card belongs in a strong routine.
+    cards:
+      - id: bi-own
+        text: Students look at duckweed and at photos of a local creek after rain, then each writes what they'd want to find out about what washes into it. Questions are conferenced for testability and kept
+        does: beginning-ideas
+        features: [swh-begin-own-question]
+      - id: bi-board
+        text: "Write the question on the board, “Does fertiliser affect duckweed growth?”, and students copy it into their books"
+        does: beginning-ideas
+        flaws: [mis-swh-supplied-question]
+      - id: t-sheet
+        text: Every group follows the same method sheet, with one cup of fertiliser and one of plain water
+        does: tests
+      - id: t-own
+        text: Groups design tests of the substances and strengths their questions named, with repeats and plain-water cups; the teacher checks each plan for safety, disposal and feasibility without redesigning it
+        does: tests
+        features: [swh-tests-follows-question]
+      - id: o-columns
+        text: Groups count fronds at the start of each lesson for two weeks, recording counts and condition in one column and what they think it means in another. Odd cups are written down
+        does: observations
+        features: [swh-obs-separate]
+      - id: o-paragraph
+        text: At the end of two weeks, groups write a paragraph on what happened in each cup, explaining as they go
+        does: observations
+      - id: c-group
+        text: Each group agrees one claim, and every member copies it into their book
+        does: claims
+      - id: c-own
+        text: Each student writes their own claim answering their own question, in a form another student could disagree with
+        does: claims
+        features: [swh-claim-arguable, swh-claim-individual]
+      - id: e-why
+        text: Students choose the counts that support their claim, say why they support it, and deal with any cup that doesn't fit. The teacher spends the lesson on this, student by student
+        does: evidence
+        features: [swh-evidence-justifies, swh-evidence-selected, swh-evidence-anomalies]
+      - id: e-table
+        text: Students copy their counting table under the Evidence heading
+        does: evidence
+        flaws: [mis-swh-evidence-recopied]
+      - id: r-key
+        text: The textbook page on algal blooms goes on the board, and students check their claims against it
+        does: reading
+        flaws: [mis-swh-answer-key]
+      - id: r-peers
+        text: Groups pair up and work out why their claims differ. Then the teacher releases a reading on nutrient run-off, blooms and salinity, and revisions are written
+        does: reading
+        features: [swh-reading-peers-first, swh-reading-not-answer-key, swh-reading-something-changes]
+      - id: rf-summary
+        text: Students write a summary of what they learned about water pollution
+        does: reflection
+        flaws: [mis-swh-lost-beginnings]
+      - id: rf-back
+        text: The lesson 1 question sheets are handed back. Students write what they first thought pollution did, what changed their mind, and what they think now
+        does: reflection
+        features: [swh-reflect-has-original, swh-begin-kept, swh-reflect-about-thinking]
+    report: [swh-begin-own-question, swh-begin-kept, swh-tests-follows-question, swh-obs-separate, swh-claim-arguable, swh-claim-individual, swh-evidence-justifies, swh-evidence-selected, swh-evidence-anomalies, swh-reading-peers-first, swh-reading-not-answer-key, swh-reading-something-changes, swh-reflect-has-original, swh-reflect-about-thinking]
+    missing:
+      beginning-ideas: mis-swh-supplied-question
+      evidence: mis-swh-evidence-recopied
+      reading: mis-swh-no-negotiation
+      reflection: mis-swh-lost-beginnings
+    hint: Read each card for what students actually do, not the heading it sounds like.
+    why: >-
+      Seven headings are easy to fill. Whether it's SWH depends on whose question it is, whether evidence says why,
+      and whether claims meet other students before they meet the textbook.
+
+  - id: cut
+    type: choice
+    at: reading
+    processing: Knowledge utilisation
+    kind: Teacher move
+    tests: design
+    vignette:
+      who: Week 7 · one lesson lost
+      text: A school event takes one of your six lessons. The routine has to fit in five.
+    q: What do you cut?
+    options:
+      - text: Drop the lesson where groups compare claims and read about run-off
+        fb: That's the cut the guide warns about most. Claims never meet anyone who might disagree, so they can stay vague at no cost.
+      - text: Fold the end-of-counting lesson into the start of lesson 5, and keep every phase
+        fb: Yes. The counting already happens in five-minute slots across the fortnight, so organising the data can share a lesson. The reading phase is the one that gets cut, and the guide says to protect it.
+      - text: Supply the question and the method, so lessons 1 and 2 become one
+        fb: You save a lesson but lose the students' own questions, so there's nothing of theirs to defend and nothing to reflect against.
+      - text: Skip the reflection and collect the books
+        fb: The beginning ideas never come back, so students never see their own change.
+    answer: 1
+    refs:
+      misapplicationIds: [mis-swh-no-negotiation]
+
+  - id: reflect
+    type: reflect
+    at: reflection
+    processing: Metacognition
+    kind: Look back
+    title: Your report, your routine.
+    q: Which look-for in your report would be hardest to meet on placement, and what would you plan differently because of it?
+    model: "Example: “Beginning ideas kept. Books go home and get lost, so I'd photograph every first page in lesson 1 and print them for lesson 6.”"
