+# SWH path, lesson 5 (bottles variant). Design level: Negotiation Day, a branching simulation of lessons 5–6 of the SWH guide's bottles sequence (swh-seq-s5-bottles). Reviewed and published by Steve on 2026-10-08.
+# Same class data as lessons 2–4.
+schemaVersion: 1
+id: swh-5-bottles
+path: swh
+track: bottles
+order: 5
+title: Negotiation Day
+summary: Run the claims, the evidence and the negotiation with a simulated Year 10 class. Every move decides whether claims meet each other before they meet the published figures.
+level: design
+version: 1.0.0
+status: published
+lastReviewed: 2026-10-08
+provenance:
+  source: ai-drafted-reviewed
+  authors: [Claude]
+  reviewedBy: [Steve Grant]
+  reviewedOn: 2026-10-08
+context:
+  stages: [stage5]
+  focusAreas: [Materials]
+  disciplines: [chemistry]
+outcomes: [SC5-MAT-01]
+builtOn:
+  sequenceIds: [swh-seq-s5-bottles]
+  lookForIds: [swh-claim-arguable, swh-evidence-justifies, swh-reading-peers-first, swh-reading-not-answer-key, swh-reflect-has-original]
+  misapplicationIds: [mis-swh-answer-key, mis-swh-evidence-recopied, mis-swh-lost-beginnings]
+guideSection: sequences
+items:
+  - id: briefing
+    type: concept
+    at: claims
+    processing: Retrieval
+    kind: How Negotiation Day works
+    title: Two lessons, five decisions
+    lead: >-
+      You're on placement with a Year 10 class recommending a reusable drink bottle for the school, near the end of
+      their SWH routine. Groups have their results, shown here. At five moments you choose what to do next, and your
+      choices decide which ideas surface, which stay hidden and which get challenged.
+    table:
+      caption: "The class's results: 750 mL bottles, 80 °C water at the start"
+      head: [Bottle, After 1 hour, Empty mass, Drop test]
+      rows:
+        - [Steel (double wall), 71 °C, 350 g, Small dent]
+        - [Plastic, 49 °C, 130 g, Undamaged]
+        - [Glass, 46 °C, 420 g, Cracked]
+        - [Aluminium, 42 °C, 160 g, Dented]
+    rows:
+      - phaseId: claims
+        does: "Lesson 5: each student writes a claim."
+        move: "Ask what it's a claim about."
+      - phaseId: evidence
+        does: "Lesson 5: each student writes why their data support it."
+        move: "Spend your time on the claim-to-data link."
+      - phaseId: reading
+        does: "Lesson 6: groups compare, then the figures arrive."
+        move: "Peers first. The figures are another voice."
+      - phaseId: reflection
+        does: "Lesson 6: beginning ideas handed back."
+        move: "Ask about their thinking."
+
+  - id: sim
+    type: sim
+    at: claims
+    processing: Knowledge utilisation
+    kind: Negotiation Day
+    tests: sim
+    cast:
+      who: Year 10 · Materials
+      text: 26 students in six groups. Each student wrote their own questions in lesson 1, so groups tested different properties.
+    sequenceId: swh-seq-s5-bottles
+    start: claim
+    nodes:
+      - id: claim
+        phase: claims
+        when: Lesson 5 · claims
+        situation: >-
+          Students are writing their claims alone. Tom's lesson 1 question was about keeping a drink hot. His claim reads
+          “Steel is the best material for a bottle.”
+        said:
+          who: Tom
+          text: “Steel's just the best. It won the cooling test.”
+          conception: 0
+        choices:
+          - text: Ask, “Best for what? Which of your lesson 1 questions does this answer?”
+            next: evidence
+            good: true
+            lookFor: swh-claim-arguable
+            surface: [0]
+            debrief: Tom rewrites it as “For keeping a drink hot, the steel bottle is the best choice.” His idea that steel is simply the best is now on the page, narrowed to a purpose, where the plastic group can push back.
+          - text: Suggest he write “Steel is the best for keeping drinks hot” instead
+            next: evidence-thin
+            good: false
+            lookFor: swh-claim-arguable
+            hide: [0]
+            debrief: The claim is better, but it's yours. Tom still thinks steel is simply the best, and he hasn't had to say what his claim is about.
+
+      - id: evidence
+        phase: evidence
+        when: Lesson 5 · evidence
+        situation: Tom starts the Evidence page by copying out his whole results table, temperatures, masses and drop results included.
+        choices:
+          - text: Sit with him and ask, “Which of these numbers makes your claim follow? Which could you leave out, and why?”
+            next: pair
+            good: true
+            lookFor: swh-evidence-justifies
+            debrief: Tom keeps the cooling results (steel lost 9 °C, the others 31 °C or more), sets mass aside because it isn't what his claim is about, and spots one steel trial that lost 20 °C. He checks, and the lid had been loose. That's the reasoning SWH exists to build.
+          - text: Let him finish. It's complete, and you need to set up for tomorrow
+            next: pair-flat
+            good: false
+            lookFor: swh-evidence-justifies
+            debrief: The page looks finished, but it never says why the data support steel. The guide warns that this lesson looks least like teaching and is the one that needs you most.
+
+      - id: evidence-thin
+        phase: evidence
+        when: Lesson 5 · evidence
+        situation: Tom's claim now carries your wording. Under Evidence he copies his results table, every number in it.
+        choices:
+          - text: Ask him to circle the numbers that make his claim follow and write one sentence on why
+            next: pair
+            good: true
+            lookFor: swh-evidence-justifies
+            surface: [0]
+            debrief: Circling the cooling results, Tom writes “steel kept it hottest”, then adds “but it's nearly three times heavier than the plastic”. His idea that steel is best at everything comes back into the open, a step later than it could have.
+          - text: Tick the page and move to the next student
+            next: pair-flat
+            good: false
+            lookFor: swh-evidence-justifies
+            debrief: A complete page with no reasoning on it. Lesson 6 starts with claims nobody has had to defend.
+
+      - id: pair
+        phase: reading
+        when: Lesson 6 · comparing claims
+        situation: Tom's steel group pairs with Aisha's group, who argued for the plastic as the bottle to carry all day. Their claims point in opposite directions.
+        said:
+          who: Aisha
+          text: “Can't we just look up the figures? They'll say who's right.”
+          conception: 1
+        choices:
+          - text: “Not yet. First write where your data agree, where they disagree, and why.”
+            next: figures
+            good: true
+            lookFor: swh-reading-peers-first
+            surface: [1]
+            challenge: [0]
+            debrief: Ten minutes in, the groups find their data don't conflict. Tom's group asked about heat, Aisha's about carrying. Nobody had to tell them there's no single best bottle.
+          - text: Hand out the published figures so they can settle it
+            next: reflect-flat
+            good: false
+            lookFor: swh-reading-peers-first
+            hide: [1]
+            debrief: The figures arrive as a verdict. Aisha's idea that the numbers decide is confirmed, and the two groups never discover why their claims differ.
+
+      - id: pair-flat
+        phase: reading
+        when: Lesson 6 · comparing claims
+        situation: Groups swap books with the bench next door. Most claims say “steel is best” or “plastic is best”, and the talk is brief.
+        said:
+          who: Aisha
+          text: “Can't we just look up the figures? They'll say who's right.”
+          conception: 1
+        choices:
+          - text: Pair the steel and plastic groups and ask each what the other's data show that theirs can't
+            next: figures
+            good: true
+            lookFor: swh-reading-peers-first
+            surface: [1]
+            challenge: [0]
+            debrief: Setting the opposite claims side by side recovers the disagreement. The groups find they weighted different properties, though you're doing more of the work than lesson 5 needed.
+          - text: Hand out the published figures so they can settle it
+            next: reflect-flat
+            good: false
+            lookFor: swh-reading-peers-first
+            hide: [1]
+            debrief: The figures decide, and claims that were never defended are crossed out or kept on the book's say-so.
+
+      - id: figures
+        phase: reading
+        when: Lesson 6 · the published figures
+        situation: Now you release thermal conductivity, density, cost and recyclability figures. Stainless steel conducts heat about 16 times better than glass and about 80 times better than the plastic.
+        said:
+          who: Tom
+          text: “So steel lets heat through easily. Our results were wrong. I'll cross out my claim.”
+          conception: 1
+        choices:
+          - text: Ask what his cooling data and the figure each show, and to look again at how the steel bottle is made
+            next: reflect
+            good: true
+            lookFor: swh-reading-not-answer-key
+            challenge: [1]
+            debrief: Tom unscrews the lid and finds the double wall. The vacuum gap is doing the work, not the steel. He keeps his data, qualifies his claim in writing, and the figure has become another voice rather than a verdict.
+          - text: Tell him to copy the published statement in place of his claim
+            next: reflect-flat
+            good: false
+            lookFor: swh-reading-not-answer-key
+            hide: [1]
+            debrief: His cooling data were real, and now they're crossed out. The class learns that testing was the slow way to reach what the book already held.
+
+      - id: reflect
+        phase: reflection
+        when: Lesson 6 · last fifteen minutes
+        situation: Claims have been revised in writing. Most of the class now argue for a bottle for a purpose.
+        choices:
+          - text: Hand back the lesson 1 question sheets and ask what they first thought a good bottle was, what moved them, and what they hold now
+            next: end
+            good: true
+            lookFor: swh-reflect-has-original
+            challenge: [0]
+            debrief: Tom reads his lesson 1 page, “Which bottle is the best?”, and writes that he now thinks it depends what the bottle is for, and that the design can matter more than the material. That sentence is the learning, in his own handwriting.
+          - text: Ask students to summarise what they've learned about bottle materials
+            next: end
+            good: false
+            lookFor: swh-reflect-has-original
+            debrief: The summaries are accurate, but they're about bottles, not about how anyone's thinking changed. The beginning ideas never came back.
+
+      - id: reflect-flat
+        phase: reflection
+        when: Lesson 6 · last fifteen minutes
+        situation: Most books now hold the published figures, copied neatly. A few claims were crossed out.
+        choices:
+          - text: Hand back the lesson 1 sheets and ask what moved their thinking, and whether it was their data or the figures
+            next: end
+            good: true
+            lookFor: swh-reflect-has-original
+            surface: [1]
+            debrief: Several students write that the figures changed their minds, not their tests. It's honest, and it gives you something to plan around next time, but the negotiation didn't happen.
+          - text: Ask students to summarise what they've learned about bottle materials
+            next: end
+            good: false
+            lookFor: swh-reflect-has-original
+            debrief: The summaries match the figures. Nobody can tell what they thought before or why it changed.
+
+    endings:
+      - minGood: 4
+        title: Claims met each other first
+        text: >-
+          Claims were tied to a purpose, evidence said why, and groups worked out their disagreement before the
+          published figures arrived as another voice. That's SWH working as the guide describes: the reading phase is
+          where the learning is.
+      - minGood: 2
+        title: Some claims were tested, some were settled for them
+        text: >-
+          Parts of the routine did their job. Look at the decisions where you supplied the wording or let the figures
+          decide. Those are the moments to plan a sentence for.
+      - minGood: 0
+        title: A practical with a textbook ending
+        text: >-
+          The class ran tests and ended up agreeing with the figures, but no claim was defended against another student.
+          The guide's look-fors for Evidence and Reading are the place to start.
+
+  - id: takeaway
+    type: choice
+    at: reading
+    processing: Analysis
+    kind: What made the difference?
+    tests: sim
+    title: In every run, one kind of move did the most damage.
+    q: Which?
+    options:
+      - text: Letting two groups reach opposite claims
+        fb: That's the point. Opposite claims from data that don't conflict are what the reading phase is built for.
+      - text: Spending lesson 5 with individual students
+        fb: That was a strong move. The guide says the claim-to-data link is where your time belongs.
+      - text: Handing back the lesson 1 sheets
+        fb: That was a strong move. Without them, students can't see their own change.
+      - text: Letting the published figures or your own wording settle a claim before students had argued it out
+        fb: Yes. Every weak path started with an authority settling something students should have defended. Each one hid an idea the routine needed to challenge.
+    answer: 3
+    refs:
+      misapplicationIds: [mis-swh-answer-key]
+
+  - id: reflect
